Rach2: let any account run ping.

Only a privileged process may open a raw ICMP socket, here as on Linux, so
upstream's ping is root's. When socket(2) refuses one with EPERM, this patch
opens an ICMP datagram socket, Linux's ping socket, and the applet follows
what that socket does: the kernel writes the identifier and the checksum,
delivers only this socket's replies, and gives them without the IPv4 header,
so the reply's TTL comes from IP_RECVTTL and the ICMPv6 filter and checksum
offset are left unset.

ping6 asks for RFC 3542's IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT in place of RFC 2292's option,
which the kernel does not keep, so that a reply's hop limit is printed. An
interval of 0, which stopped the interval timer and with it the requests and
the check of -w, becomes the shortest the timer takes.

